WEBVTT

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:06.130
Could a share of daily sales repay
this equipment repair in 120 days?

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:12.730
I selected Local Windows and generated
750 simulated merchants.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.160
I submitted one lending policy.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.660
The run evaluated every application
under both Baseline and Recession Energy.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:24.440
About two hours later,
the financial checks passed.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.850
The console listed this run’s
Tableau and Power BI workbooks.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.600
Tableau pairs each merchant’s
two decisions.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.690
115 businesses received financing
in Baseline.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.250
35 also received financing under stress.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.930
Power BI shows both contracts
for merchant 462.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:51.400
In each, the business received $146,600,

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.650
with the same repayment terms.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:59.440
Just under ten cents of each eligible
sales dollar went toward repayment.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.140
On day three, zero eligible sales
meant a zero payment.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.360
For 60 days, the balances match.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.800
Then the model starts replaying sales
dates affected by the energy shock.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.550
Baseline pays off on day 111.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.060
Under stress, over $3,700 is still due
on day 120.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.630
All 35 merchants funded in both scenarios
paid off in Baseline.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.120
Under stress, 34 did.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.190
This equipment repair merchant
accounts for the difference.

